1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:11,600
Okay, moving on to urinary tract infection, let's just remind ourselves that we've got this urethra, which is draining the bladder, and of course, what feeds into the bladder are the ureters, and those are coming off the kidney.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:17,680
So, in urinary tract infection, we can have infection of the urethra, the bladder, or the kidneys.

3
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:26,060
Now, most commonly, when you get infection of any of these regions, it's usually due to an ascending infection, so it starts in the urethra, and it works its way up.

4
00:00:26,060 --> 00:00:33,760
This helps to explain why there's an increased incidence in females. It also helps to explain why the risk factors include, for example, sexual intercourse.

5
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:38,220
With sexual intercourse, there's an increased risk of introducing bacteria into this tract.

6
00:00:38,820 --> 00:00:43,980
Urinary stasis, whenever you have stasis of urine, there'll be an increased risk for bacteria to grow in that urine.

7
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:51,100
And catheters, because if you have a catheter, that provides a conduit for bacteria to actually get into this pathway.

8
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:55,560
Now, when you have inflammation of the bladder, that is called cystitis.

9
00:00:56,060 --> 00:01:04,140
And cystitis will present with dysuria, which is pain with urination, urinary frequency, urgency, and suprapubic pain.

10
00:01:04,680 --> 00:01:09,120
And one of the interesting things about cystitis is that systemic signs are usually absent.

11
00:01:09,580 --> 00:01:12,340
With that background in mind, let's go ahead and read what we have in the text.

12
00:01:12,340 --> 00:01:15,980
So, urinary tract infection is infection of the urethral, bladder, or kidney.

13
00:01:16,460 --> 00:01:20,560
Most commonly arises due to ascending infection, increased risk in females.

14
00:01:21,040 --> 00:01:24,420
One of the risk factors, sexual intercourse, urinary stasis, and catheters.

15
00:01:24,420 --> 00:01:31,800
When you have inflammation of the bladder, that's called cystitis, it presents with dysuria, urinary frequency, urgency, suprapubic pain.

16
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:34,260
You really don't have systemic signs.

17
00:01:34,380 --> 00:01:36,720
You're not going to have fevers, chills, night sweats, etc.

18
00:01:37,340 --> 00:01:41,600
Now, lab findings are particularly helpful in making the diagnosis of cystitis.

19
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:46,820
The urinalysis will show cloudy urine with greater than 10 white blood cells per high power field.

20
00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:55,380
The urine dipstick will show positive leukocyte esterase, and of course, that's due to the fact that you've got pus in the urine, and one of the enzymes would be leukocyte esterase.

21
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:57,240
In addition, there'll be nitrites.

22
00:01:57,700 --> 00:02:02,280
Now, bacteria, they take nitrates, which are present in the urine, and convert it to nitrites.

23
00:02:02,400 --> 00:02:07,840
And so, when you have nitrites, that tells you that you have bacteria within the urine.

24
00:02:07,840 --> 00:02:13,700
And finally, the gold standard is culture greater than 100,000 colony-forming units.

25
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:15,200
That's the gold standard.

26
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:17,420
The etiologies include E. coli.

27
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:19,740
This is the vast majority of cystitis.

28
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:24,840
Staph saprophyticus can be particularly seen in young, sexually active women.

29
00:02:25,160 --> 00:02:28,480
However, even in this demographic, E. coli is the most common cause.

30
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,120
Other causes include Klebsiella pneumoniae.

31
00:02:31,120 --> 00:02:38,840
A proteus is interesting, because when it causes a cystitis, you get an alkaline urine, which also has an ammonia scent, and so that's important.

32
00:02:39,280 --> 00:02:42,860
And then enterococcus fecalus can also result in cystitis.

33
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:52,240
Now, if a patient presents with sterile pyuria, which basically means that they have pyuria, but they have a negative urine culture, then you want to think about urethritis.

34
00:02:52,680 --> 00:02:58,560
Now, remember that if they have pyuria, you'll see greater than 10 white blood cells per high-power field in the urinalysis,

35
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:03,280
and you'll see leukocyte esterase on the urine dipstick, but the culture would be negative.

36
00:03:03,380 --> 00:03:07,080
And remember, the culture was the gold standard for making the diagnosis of cystitis.

37
00:03:07,460 --> 00:03:11,980
So when you have pyuria, but you have a negative culture, you're thinking about urethritis.

38
00:03:12,340 --> 00:03:16,660
And that's important, because the key organisms then become chlamydia and Neisseria.

39
00:03:17,460 --> 00:03:19,420
Pylinephritis is when you get infection of the kidney.

40
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:22,940
Now, this is also usually going to be due to an ascending infection,

41
00:03:23,300 --> 00:03:27,440
and there'll be an increased risk if there's reflux from the bladder up to the ureters.

42
00:03:27,440 --> 00:03:33,620
Of course, if what's in the bladder is being refluxed into the kidney, there'll be an increased risk for infection to go up to the kidney.

43
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:38,040
Now, patients with pyelonephritis do present with systemic signs, so you get fever.

44
00:03:38,520 --> 00:03:46,080
And in addition, you get flank pain, because the inflammation results in sensitization of the nerves of the capsule of the kidney,

45
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:48,040
and so that would result in flank pain.

46
00:03:48,320 --> 00:03:51,220
You get white blood cell casts and leukocytosis.

47
00:03:51,220 --> 00:03:58,120
Now, the reason you get the white blood cell casts is because when the infection ascends up from the ureter into the kidney,

48
00:03:58,400 --> 00:04:01,040
it's going to be walking its way up the tubule.

49
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:06,800
And as it walks its way up the tubule, that's going to result in inflammatory cells coming into the tubule,

50
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:10,780
and those inflammatory cells will cast themselves in the shape of the tubule,

51
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:15,460
so that when this is urinated out, you're actually going to get a white blood cell cast.

52
00:04:15,460 --> 00:04:19,420
And that's an important finding in pyelonephritis.

53
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:27,020
In addition, you'll have symptoms of cystitis as well, and again, that's because the infection usually ascends from the bladder up into the kidney.

54
00:04:27,440 --> 00:04:32,680
The most common pathogens for pyelonephritis include E. coli, Klebsiella can also do it,

55
00:04:32,940 --> 00:04:36,200
and Enterococcus can also work its way up into the kidney.

56
00:04:36,640 --> 00:04:43,820
Chronic pyelonephritis is when you get interstitial fibrosis and atrophy of the tubules due to multiple bouts of acute pyelonephritis.

57
00:04:43,820 --> 00:04:50,740
Now, it's classically seen in a child that has vesicoureteral reflux, or VUR, or it can also be seen with obstruction.

58
00:04:51,140 --> 00:04:52,920
Let's just talk about VUR for a moment.

59
00:04:53,200 --> 00:04:56,720
Here's the bladder, and recall that the ureters, of course, plug into the bladder,

60
00:04:57,020 --> 00:04:59,180
and they plug in at a very particular angle.

61
00:04:59,540 --> 00:05:04,260
This angle helps to prevent the reflux of urine back up into the ureter.

62
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:08,940
However, if there's a malformation of this angle or of this apparatus,

63
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:13,100
there can be an increased risk of reflux up into the kidney,

64
00:05:13,420 --> 00:05:18,940
and the reflux up into the kidney could then result in chronic scarring and interstitial fibrosis

65
00:05:18,940 --> 00:05:22,320
with atrophy of the tubules, which is called chronic pyelonephritis.

66
00:05:22,840 --> 00:05:28,820
Now, on gross exam, in chronic pyelonephritis, the kidneys will show cortical scarring and blunted calyces.

67
00:05:29,380 --> 00:05:35,320
Now, one of the characteristic patterns of scarring is when you have scarring at the upper pole and lower pole,

68
00:05:35,320 --> 00:05:37,880
and that's characteristic of VUR.

69
00:05:38,840 --> 00:05:44,940
Now, the histology in chronic pyelonephritis will show atrophic tubules that are loaded with protonaceous material,

70
00:05:45,400 --> 00:05:51,100
and this protonaceous material is reminiscent of the colloid that would be present within thyroid follicles,

71
00:05:51,560 --> 00:05:54,400
and hence they call that thyroidization of the kidney.

72
00:05:54,800 --> 00:05:55,620
Here's a picture.

73
00:05:55,820 --> 00:06:00,500
You can see that these tubules are loaded with all this pink material that resembles colloid,

74
00:06:00,500 --> 00:06:08,360
and so, therefore, these mimic thyroid follicles, and hence the term thyroidization of the kidney.

75
00:06:09,620 --> 00:06:13,240
Nepherolithiasis is when you get precipitation of a urinary solute as a stone.

76
00:06:13,620 --> 00:06:16,720
Now, recall that there are many different solutes that are present in the urine.

77
00:06:17,120 --> 00:06:22,980
If that solute achieves a high enough concentration that it can precipitate, that can then result in a stone.

78
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:28,420
So the risk factors would be high concentration of the solute, as I've just explained, or low urine volume,

79
00:06:28,420 --> 00:06:33,240
and, of course, low urine volume would result in an increased concentration of the solute.

80
00:06:33,580 --> 00:06:38,220
Now, the classic presentation of nephrolithiasis is that you get colicky pain with hematuria

81
00:06:38,220 --> 00:06:44,380
and unilateral flank tenderness as you try to pass that stone through the ureters.

82
00:06:44,820 --> 00:06:50,080
Now, the stone is usually passed within hours, but if it's not, then surgical intervention may be required.

83
00:06:50,540 --> 00:06:54,780
This table highlights some of the key types of stones and associated high-yield points,

84
00:06:55,100 --> 00:06:57,460
and so we're going to spend a couple of moments going over it.

85
00:06:57,460 --> 00:07:01,700
Now, one common type of stone, in fact, the most common type of stone seen in adults,

86
00:07:02,020 --> 00:07:05,180
is a calcium oxalate or a calcium phosphate stone.

87
00:07:05,600 --> 00:07:09,960
Now, if a patient has a calcium stone, then obviously there was high calcium within the urine,

88
00:07:10,360 --> 00:07:15,300
and so one of the very important things that you have to exclude in these patients is hypercalcemia,

89
00:07:15,640 --> 00:07:19,800
because, of course, if there's a high calcium in the urine, it may have come from a high calcium in the blood.

90
00:07:20,400 --> 00:07:26,340
However, it's important to note that actually the most common cause of calcium stone is not hypercalcemia,

91
00:07:26,340 --> 00:07:29,780
but instead something called idiopathic hypercalcemia,

92
00:07:30,100 --> 00:07:34,200
where, for some reason, the patients actually put excess calcium into the urine,

93
00:07:34,560 --> 00:07:37,380
but the calcium level in the blood is actually normal.

94
00:07:37,780 --> 00:07:41,100
Now, you can also see a calcium oxalate stone with Crohn's disease,

95
00:07:41,440 --> 00:07:46,860
and that's because the small bowel damage in Crohn's disease results in increased resorption of oxalate,

96
00:07:47,120 --> 00:07:50,240
which can then bind calcium, creating a calcium oxalate stone.

97
00:07:50,240 --> 00:07:54,340
The treatment for this type of stone would be hydrochlorothiazine.

98
00:07:54,660 --> 00:07:59,700
This is a calcium-sparing diuretic and would decrease the amount of calcium going into the urine,

99
00:08:00,140 --> 00:08:02,780
decreasing the risk for future development of stones.

100
00:08:03,460 --> 00:08:09,680
The second most common type of stone is an ammonium-magnesium-phosphate stone, also called an AMP stone.

101
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:14,880
The most common cause of this type of stone is infection with urease-positive organisms,

102
00:08:14,880 --> 00:08:21,060
such as Proteus or Klebsiella, and these organisms result in alkalinization of the urine,

103
00:08:21,380 --> 00:08:23,140
which then leads to formation of the stone.

104
00:08:23,660 --> 00:08:29,040
Now, it's very high yield to note that this classically results in a staghorn colliculus in the renal calyces.

105
00:08:29,540 --> 00:08:33,400
Now, this would not normally be passed, and therefore, it would have to be surgically removed.

106
00:08:33,740 --> 00:08:37,500
And one of the key complications is that this stone can act as a nidus for UTI,

107
00:08:37,500 --> 00:08:42,520
and so the patients can develop urinary tract infections because of the presence of this stone.

108
00:08:42,620 --> 00:08:44,760
So, again, it needs to be surgically removed.

109
00:08:45,100 --> 00:08:48,080
In addition, you're going to have to treat the organism that caused it,

110
00:08:48,140 --> 00:08:52,720
whether it be Proteus or Klebsiella, to prevent future recurrence of such a stone.

111
00:08:53,540 --> 00:08:56,800
The third most common type of stone is a uric acid stone.

112
00:08:57,140 --> 00:09:01,620
Now, this stone is radiolucent, which means that it would not be visible on x-ray,

113
00:09:01,620 --> 00:09:05,280
and that's important because the other types of stones that we just talked about,

114
00:09:05,280 --> 00:09:09,060
the calcium stone and the AMP stone, would be seen on x-ray.

115
00:09:09,540 --> 00:09:15,960
Now, the risk factors for the uric acid stone are hot, arid climates, low urine volume, and acidic pH.

116
00:09:16,180 --> 00:09:19,500
All of these things increase the risk of uric acid stones.

117
00:09:19,920 --> 00:09:23,180
This is the most common type of stone seen in patients with gout.

118
00:09:23,500 --> 00:09:26,080
Again, uric acid is associated with gout.

119
00:09:26,420 --> 00:09:30,900
And then, it's also seen in patients with hyperuricemia or myeloproliferative disorders.

120
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:34,100
Now, hyperuricemia could be seen, for example, in leukemia,

121
00:09:34,100 --> 00:09:36,940
where you're generating a lot of cells, and a lot of cells are turning over,

122
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:38,580
and so a lot of nucleus would turn over.

123
00:09:38,960 --> 00:09:41,140
And it can also be seen in myeloproliferative disorders.

124
00:09:41,300 --> 00:09:44,460
Again, in myeloproliferative disorders, you're generating a lot of cells,

125
00:09:44,560 --> 00:09:46,100
and so there's a lot of nuclear turnover,

126
00:09:46,480 --> 00:09:49,660
and so that can also result in high levels of uric acid.

127
00:09:50,140 --> 00:09:52,400
Now, treatment of this type of stone involves hydration

128
00:09:52,400 --> 00:09:56,400
and alkalinization of the urine, for example, with potassium bicarbonate.

129
00:09:56,580 --> 00:10:00,020
Now, again, remember that acidic pH helps to form this type of stone,

130
00:10:00,020 --> 00:10:03,020
so an alkaline urine would protect against formation.

131
00:10:03,700 --> 00:10:07,400
Allopurinol can also be used in patients with gout to decrease the uric acid.

132
00:10:08,220 --> 00:10:10,520
The fourth and final type of stone is a cysteine stone.

133
00:10:10,840 --> 00:10:13,280
This is a very rare cause of nephrolithiasis.

134
00:10:13,400 --> 00:10:16,720
It's seen in children, and it's classically associated with cystinuria,

135
00:10:16,880 --> 00:10:20,600
where you have a genetic defect of the tubules so that you don't resorb cysteine.

136
00:10:20,980 --> 00:10:23,740
If you don't resorb cysteine, cysteine piles up in the filtrate,

137
00:10:24,040 --> 00:10:25,920
increasing the risk for a cysteine stone.

138
00:10:25,920 --> 00:10:30,540
Now, very important to note that this can also produce a staghorn colliculus.

139
00:10:30,940 --> 00:10:34,200
So, a staghorn colliculus in an adult, you're thinking AMP stone.

140
00:10:34,580 --> 00:10:38,000
However, a staghorn colliculus in a kid, you're thinking a cysteine stone.

141
00:10:38,440 --> 00:10:43,020
And the treatment can involve hydration and alkalinization of the urine as well.

142
00:10:43,600 --> 00:10:46,780
Moving on to chronic renal failure, this is end-stage kidney failure.

143
00:10:47,100 --> 00:10:49,500
Important to note that you can get end-stage kidney failure

144
00:10:49,500 --> 00:10:51,180
from any of the compartments of the kidney.

145
00:10:51,520 --> 00:10:54,840
For example, if you damage the glomeruli, you can get end-stage kidney failure,

146
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:59,300
as we saw in nephrotic syndrome or rapidly progressive glomerulonephritis.

147
00:10:59,880 --> 00:11:03,000
If you damage the tubules, you can get end-stage renal failure.

148
00:11:04,200 --> 00:11:06,920
Inflammatory diseases can result in end-stage renal failure,

149
00:11:07,320 --> 00:11:11,000
and damage to the blood vessels can also result in end-stage renal failure.

150
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:17,460
The three most common causes of chronic renal failure are diabetes, hypertension, and glomerular disease.

151
00:11:18,120 --> 00:11:20,420
Now, when patients develop end-stage kidney failure,

152
00:11:20,740 --> 00:11:24,080
they are going to have several clinical features associated with that.

153
00:11:24,080 --> 00:11:25,660
The first is uremia.

154
00:11:26,060 --> 00:11:29,040
And uremia is a clinical constellation of symptoms

155
00:11:29,040 --> 00:11:32,520
that are based on increased nitrogenous waste products in the blood.

156
00:11:32,880 --> 00:11:35,920
For example, the patients will have nausea, anorexia,

157
00:11:36,220 --> 00:11:37,700
they can develop pericarditis,

158
00:11:38,040 --> 00:11:39,300
they get platelet dysfunction,

159
00:11:39,500 --> 00:11:43,940
and that's because the uremia inhibits both platelet adhesion and platelet aggregation.

160
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:46,820
They can get encephalopathy with asterixis,

161
00:11:47,240 --> 00:11:50,540
and then they can get deposition of urea crystals in the skin.

162
00:11:50,540 --> 00:11:53,320
And all of this is sort of the constellation of uremia,

163
00:11:53,740 --> 00:11:56,900
which is due to the increased nitrogenous waste products in the blood.

164
00:11:57,560 --> 00:12:00,460
In addition, patients can also develop salt and water retention.

165
00:12:00,900 --> 00:12:03,840
And when there's salt and water retention, of course, there will be hypertension.

166
00:12:04,480 --> 00:12:08,480
Patients can also develop hyperkalemia because they're not able to excrete potassium,

167
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:12,980
along with a metabolic acidosis because they're not able to get rid of organic acids.

168
00:12:12,980 --> 00:12:16,560
And so this would be, again, an anion gap, metabolic acidosis.

169
00:12:17,100 --> 00:12:20,840
Anemia can also be seen, and that's because the kidney normally makes erythropoietin,

170
00:12:20,900 --> 00:12:24,160
and so if you get damage to the kidney, you might get less erythropoietin.

171
00:12:24,560 --> 00:12:26,660
One of the things that board examiners like you to know

172
00:12:26,660 --> 00:12:31,780
is that erythropoietin is produced by the renal peritubular interstitial cells.

173
00:12:32,260 --> 00:12:35,100
The renal peritubular interstitial cells.

174
00:12:35,520 --> 00:12:36,980
Patients can also have hypocalcemia.

175
00:12:37,660 --> 00:12:39,840
There's two main reasons for the hypocalcemia.

176
00:12:39,840 --> 00:12:44,160
Number one, because there's decreased 1-alpha-hydroxylation of vitamin D.

177
00:12:44,460 --> 00:12:47,720
Recall that vitamin D is 1-alpha-hydroxylated by the kidney

178
00:12:47,720 --> 00:12:49,860
in the proximal renal tubular cells.

179
00:12:50,360 --> 00:12:52,860
And then number two, because the kidney is failing,

180
00:12:52,980 --> 00:12:54,760
it's not able to excrete phosphate,

181
00:12:55,160 --> 00:12:58,520
and if you can't excrete phosphate, you're going to get hyperphosphatemia.

182
00:12:58,640 --> 00:13:01,320
Now remember that the high phosphate will bind with calcium,

183
00:13:01,760 --> 00:13:04,780
and so therefore the free calcium will become low,

184
00:13:05,120 --> 00:13:08,280
which is another reason why patients develop hypocalcemia.

185
00:13:08,280 --> 00:13:11,080
Finally, they can develop renal osteodystrophy.

186
00:13:11,520 --> 00:13:15,060
Now renal osteodystrophy is a damage to the bone

187
00:13:15,060 --> 00:13:16,760
that occurs because of renal failure.

188
00:13:17,080 --> 00:13:19,080
And there's actually three independent components.

189
00:13:19,600 --> 00:13:22,320
The first is that you get osteitis fibrosis cystica.

190
00:13:22,740 --> 00:13:26,220
Now the idea here is that the patients have hypocalcemia.

191
00:13:26,360 --> 00:13:30,280
And because they have hypocalcemia, the PTH levels will rise,

192
00:13:30,600 --> 00:13:33,300
and when the PTH levels rise, that will hit the bone,

193
00:13:33,620 --> 00:13:35,900
and eventually it will cause resorption of calcium.

194
00:13:35,900 --> 00:13:39,180
Now as you get resorption of calcium, you're eventually going to burn out the bone,

195
00:13:39,640 --> 00:13:43,740
and as you burn out and destroy the bone, you get fibrosis and cyst formation.

196
00:13:44,200 --> 00:13:46,780
And so that's called osteitis fibrosa cystica.

197
00:13:47,260 --> 00:13:49,940
Now at the same time, the patients will also have osteomalacia.

198
00:13:50,400 --> 00:13:51,560
Now what is osteomalacia?

199
00:13:51,760 --> 00:13:56,680
This is when you cannot mineralize the osteoid made by the osteoblast.

200
00:13:57,320 --> 00:13:59,680
So let's take a step back, remind ourselves that the osteoblast,

201
00:14:00,480 --> 00:14:03,680
they lay down new bone, but they don't lay down bone, they lay down osteoid.

202
00:14:03,680 --> 00:14:07,780
And that osteoid then needs to be mineralized with calcium and phosphate.

203
00:14:08,440 --> 00:14:11,240
However, if the patients don't have good vitamin D function,

204
00:14:11,620 --> 00:14:13,500
their calcium levels will be low,

205
00:14:13,900 --> 00:14:16,540
and hence they won't be able to mineralize that new bone.

206
00:14:16,960 --> 00:14:19,360
And so therefore, they'll develop osteomalacia.

207
00:14:19,840 --> 00:14:22,380
And finally, they can develop osteoporosis.

208
00:14:22,760 --> 00:14:26,280
And that's when you leach calcium from the bone slowly over time.

209
00:14:26,760 --> 00:14:30,860
Now the reason why they get osteoporosis is because they have a metabolic acidosis.

210
00:14:30,860 --> 00:14:35,240
And one of the buffering systems for acidosis is to buffer it against the bone.

211
00:14:35,580 --> 00:14:38,480
When you buffer it against the bone, that leaches calcium from the bone.

212
00:14:38,800 --> 00:14:41,580
And the slow leaching of calcium from the bone over time

213
00:14:41,580 --> 00:14:44,940
would then result in osteoporosis, or parotic bone.

214
00:14:45,540 --> 00:14:50,020
Now patients with end-stage renal failure are going to require dialysis or renal transplant.

215
00:14:50,540 --> 00:14:54,460
Important to note that when patients have end-stage renal failure, the kidneys are shrunken.

216
00:14:54,460 --> 00:14:58,580
If you then put that kidney on dialysis, cysts will develop.

217
00:14:58,920 --> 00:15:01,880
So often, patients on dialysis will develop cysts of their kidney.

218
00:15:02,220 --> 00:15:05,700
However, they will have a shrunken kidney upon which cysts develop.

219
00:15:06,020 --> 00:15:09,000
So it's different than, for example, a polycystic kidney disease

220
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:10,980
where you have big kidneys with cysts.

221
00:15:11,020 --> 00:15:13,160
These would be shrunken kidneys with cysts.

222
00:15:13,180 --> 00:15:16,280
And they, of course, would occur in the context of end-stage renal failure.

223
00:15:16,780 --> 00:15:19,200
Superhigh yield to note that patients who are on dialysis

224
00:15:19,200 --> 00:15:22,820
have an increased risk for the development of renal cell carcinoma

225
00:15:22,820 --> 00:15:25,320
in that shrunken end-stage kidney.

226
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:27,180
Again, that's superhigh yield to note.

227
00:15:27,600 --> 00:15:30,120
And that concludes this particular series of sections.

